  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in New Mexico through FY2027 to date shows concentrated spending and a sharp year-over-year drop. Total obligations are $1.6B, across 504 awards with an average award size of $3.1M. Obligations are down 90.0% year over year. The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer with $1.5B in obligations, leaving much smaller totals for other agencies such as the Department of Veterans Affairs at $19.8M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $17M. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligation at $1.5B, reflecting the Department of Energy work. TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC are the top vendors with $875M and $633M respectively. Historical fiscal-year obligations show a peak in FY26 at $15.8B and a much smaller FY27 total of $1.6B so far, which indicates the current-year totals are partial. Readers should treat FY2027 numbers as fiscal year to date and note that the note specifies current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in New Mexico this year?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy obligated $1.5B, concentrating the majority of the $1.6B total. Facilities Support Services accounted for $1.5B of obligations, matching the Department of Energy scale and indicating a tight clustering of dollars in one agency and one industry."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2027 to date compare with recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY26 obligations were $15.8B, while FY27 obligations are $1.6B to date. Obligations are down 90.0% year over year, and the FY2027 totals are partial (fiscal year to date)."
      }
    ]
  },
